/// How far before the reported range the "first seen" scans may look.
///
/// These bounds are what keep retention runnable. Both scans previously had no
/// time predicate at all -- they read every `event_category = 'app'` row ever
/// written on each request -- so as telemetry grew the query crossed the 15s
/// `statement_timeout` and the endpoint started returning 500s.
///
/// Bounding is safe because the scans only exist to classify new vs returning
/// *inside* the reported range. For any day D in the range:
///   - a first event inside the range is measured exactly, and
///   - a first event anywhere before `from` makes the row returning for every
///     day in the range, regardless of how much earlier it actually was.
/// So the lookback only has to be long enough to notice that earlier activity
/// exists at all.
///
/// Sessions rotate every six hours (`AppTelemetryConfig.sessionRotation`), so a
/// session cannot meaningfully predate the range by more than that; a week is
/// already far past generous. Users need a real window, since "returning user"
/// is a genuine all-time property -- a year covers any active account, and a
/// user with no activity for over a year reads as new, which is the more useful
/// reading of a reactivation anyway.
